  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10c-catfishes-remaining-catfish-from-south-america/centromochlus-heckelii-2/",
      "html": "<p>This interesting driftwood catfish is only rarely available and in small numbers. It reaches a maximum length of about 14-15 cm. Two features are very obvious: the large eyes and the large pectoral fins, which are always spread.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/auchenipteridae/centromochlus_heckelii.htm",
      "html": "<p>Jeremy Basch of AmazonTropics.com, an expert on these catfishes, states that unfortunately this species is not really a good aquarium candidate because of its constant swimming.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10c-catfishes-remaining-catfish-from-south-america/centromochlus-heckelii-2/",
      "html": "<p>The species is widely distributed in the Amazon and Orinoco river basins. The specimens described here originate from Peru, which is also the type locality of the species.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/auchenipteridae/centromochlus_heckelii.htm",
      "html": "<p>South America: Amazon and Orinoco River basins. Type locality: Marañon River, upper Amazon and Napo Rivers.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/auchenipteridae/centromochlus_heckelii.htm",
      "html": "<p>One might see better results in a tank with a constant stream of water that goes across the tank. This would require a fairly large pump or many jet lifters.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/auchenipteridae/centromochlus_heckelii.htm",
      "html": "<p>In its native habitat they feed on small invertebrates and crustaceans. In the aquarium they will eat anything given, such as frozen bloodworm, catfish tablets, white worm (sparingly), prawns and shrimp.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/auchenipteridae/centromochlus_heckelii.htm",
      "html": "<p>This species constantly swims in the aquarium and seems to be unable to detect the sides of the tank. It never goes and hides; they literally swim 99% of the time. They are only occasionally seen resting on the ground, which is uncommon.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10c-catfishes-remaining-catfish-from-south-america/centromochlus-heckelii-2/",
      "html": "<p>The sexes can be distinguished best by the shape of the maxillary barbels: these barbels are curved in males and straight in females.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/auchenipteridae/centromochlus_heckelii.htm",
      "html": "<p>The sexes can be distinguished best by the shape of the maxillary barbels: these barbels are curved in males and straight in females.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10c-catfishes-remaining-catfish-from-south-america/centromochlus-heckelii-2/",
      "html": "<p>The spines of the pectoral fins should be handled with care: one can be stung nastily by them, and the spines also tend to tangle in the mesh of a net when the fish has to be caught.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/auchenipteridae/centromochlus_heckelii.htm",
      "html": "<p>Mees (1974) assigned this species to Tatia but Ferraris (2007) reverted it to the original genus Centromochlus.</p><p>Etymology: The genus name Centromochlus: kentron, thorn or spine; mochlus, lever or crowbar, presumably referring to the strongly serrated dorsal-fin spine. The specific name heckelii: in honour of Johann Jakob Heckel (1790-1857), curator of fishes, Naturhistorisches Museum in Vienna.</p><p>Two features of the fish are very obvious: the large eyes and the large pectoral fins, which are always spread. The pectoral-fin spines should be handled with care as one can be stung by them, and the spines also tend to tangle in the mesh of a net when the fish needs to be caught.</p><p>IUCN Red List: despite there being little information available on this species, it is assessed as Least Concern due to its wide range and no direct threat (IUCN 2023).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/auchenipteridae/centromochlus_heckelii.htm",
      "html": "<p>Basch, Jeremy. amazontropics.com pers. comm. 2021. Ferraris, C.J. Jr., 2007. Checklist of catfishes, recent and fossil (Osteichthyes: Siluriformes), and catalogue of siluriform primary types. Zootaxa 1418:1-628. Frederico, R.G. 2023. Centromochlus heckelii. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2023. Froese, R. and D. Pauly. Editors. 2019. FishBase. World Wide Web electronic publication. www.fishbase.org, ( 08/2019 ). Soares-Porto, L.M., 1998. Monophyly and interrelationships of the Centromochlinae (Siluriformes: Auchenipteridae). p. 331-350. In L.R. Malabarba, R.E. Reis, R.P. Vari, Z.M.S. Lucena and C.A.S. Lucena (eds.) Phylogeny and classification of neotropical fishes. Porto Alegre: EDIPUCRS. www.aquariumglaser.de (Frank Schäfer).</p>"
    }
  ],
